00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.550
that President Trump is somehow eligible

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.266
to run for a third term as President.

00:00:15.266 --> 00:00:17.504
- I'd be for a third
term in heartbeat, yeah.

00:00:17.504 --> 00:00:20.260
- If we could put him on
another third term, we got him.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.980
- Pundits have jumped in,

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.780
and even the President
himself has suggested

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.840
that he might be eligible to get more

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:27.940
than eight years in office.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.520
- And then do you want to
really drive them crazy?

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.250
Go to #thirdterm #fourthterm.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.110
You'll drive them totally crazy.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.280
- On multiple occasions.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.510
- And if things keep
going like they're going,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.060
we'll go and we'll do what we have to do.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.180
We'll do a three and four and a five.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.590
- Of course, we all
learned in grade school

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.210
that President's are limited to two terms.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.080
But is there a clever way
around it, through, perhaps,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.450
impeachment or an end run
around the vice presidency?

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.330
Well, I took a deep dive into this

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.660
just because I was curious,
and also terrified.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.160
So you don't have to.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.080
So stick around and let's
figure out if there's a way

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.108
to get more than eight years
in office as President.

00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:10.525
(grand orchestral music)

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.960
Hey, LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.810
because Jerry Falwell, Jr.
suggested that President Trump

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.926
should add two years to his
presidency to make up for

00:01:18.926 --> 00:01:22.717
the indignity of the Mueller
investigation, tweeting,

00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:25.347
"After the best week ever
for @realDonaldTrummp -

00:01:25.347 --> 00:01:27.657
"no obstruction, no collusion,
New York Time admits

00:01:27.657 --> 00:01:29.777
"Barack Obama did spy on his campaign,

00:01:29.777 --> 00:01:30.947
"and the economy is soaring.

00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:33.907
"I now support reparations -
Trump should have two years

00:01:33.907 --> 00:01:36.777
"added to his first term
as pay back for time stolen

00:01:36.777 --> 00:01:38.340
"by this corrupt failed coup."

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.260
Adding fuel to the fire,

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.020
President Trump has sometimes speculated

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.180
on whether he could seek a third term.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.950
- Under the normal rules,
I'll be out in 2024.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.850
So we may have to go for an extra term.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.960
- Now, the 22nd Amendment
limits a President

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.580
to two terms in office.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.390
But other President's have also mentioned

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.470
serving a little bit longer.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.610
During his second term in 1987,

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.570
Ronald Reagan said maybe
there should be a movement

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.670
to repeal to the 22nd Amendment.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.830
And the grandiosity is not
limited to Republicans.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:09.030
Barack Obama said that he
thought he could be elected

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.940
a third time if he was eligible to run.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.440
And all this poses the question,

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.850
can a President serve more
than eight years in office?

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.220
Now, to answer this question we have to go

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.210
all the way back to the Revolutionary War.

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.210
The framers seriously
considered making the office

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.560
of presidency a lifetime appointment.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.370
And although they didn't
go through with this plan,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.840
they were still reluctant to limit

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.720
the President's time in office.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.780
As a result, there were no term limits

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:31.770
in the version of the Constitution

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.530
that was ratified in 1788.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.230
The final version of the
Constitution ratified by the states

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.770
made the office of President
a four year position.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.230
The Constitution also granted
the office of Vice President

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:42.700
who would be the President's successor

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.670
in the event the President
could no longer serve.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.730
Of course, at the time
the Constitution specified

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.983
that the person who finished second

00:02:48.983 --> 00:02:50.770
in the Presidential election
would win the vice presidency,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.790
which was a terrible idea that
had horrible repercussions

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.810
that I'll probably cover in another video.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.610
Now, George Washington was the only man

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.360
ever seriously considered for the first

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.920
President of the United States.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.020
Everyone assumed he
would win in a landslide.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.990
And he did twice.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.790
This is probably why many
people would have been happy

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.470
to make Washington President for life.

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:09.970
Or an elected king.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:12.820
Luckily, Washington was not
okay serving as President

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.030
for life, so when he ran
he privately assured people

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.570
that he wouldn't even serve
a full four-year term.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.760
And he confided in Alexander Hamilton

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.720
that he thought he would
only serve for two years

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.440
and get everything straightened
out and then retire early.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.640
Though Washington did
run for President twice

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.050
and was elected twice,
he decided to retire

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.000
after the end of his second term.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.660
This move set a precedent
that lasted 150 years.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.660
Washington's retirement
established a custom

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.810
that President's followed
until the 20th Century

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:42.050
when Franklin Delano Roosevelt
was elected four times.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:44.560
This is why customs
and laws are different.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.280
A custom is defined as a traditional

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.580
and widely accepted way of
behaving or doing something

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.600
that is specific to a particular
society, place, or time.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.930
A custom is an accepted norm
that cannot be enforced by law.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.820
By contrast, laws, as you know,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.180
are systems of rules enacted
by a governing authority

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.550
which can enforce them through penalties.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.050
And although most President's
followed Washington's custom

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.850
of quitting after two terms,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.700
some of them tried for a third term.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.390
Ulysses S. Grant ran for
a third term in 1880,

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.160
but lost to James Garfield
in the Republican primary.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:14.800
Grover Cleveland wanted a third term,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.390
but nobody wanted him.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:18.300
Now, Teddy Roosevelt
served as Vice President

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.700
for James McKinley and
became President in 1902

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.620
after McKinley was assassinated.

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.900
Now, T. Dog was re-elected in 1904

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.020
and served a full four-year term.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.250
But in 1912 he ran for a
third non-consecutive term,

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.800
this time as nominee of
the Progressive Party.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.670
But he lost to Woodrow Wilson.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.080
Wilson himself sought a third term,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.850
but the Democratic Party
declined to nominate him.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:41.740
Enter Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.320
Franklin Roosevelt is the only
person elected for President

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:45.850
for more than two terms.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:47.500
He succeeded in getting a third term

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.960
because he was President during a time

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.210
of unprecedented crisis for America,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:54.180
both the Great Depression,
and World War II.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.940
- I will, with God's help,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.178
continue to serve with
the best of my ability

00:05:01.178 --> 00:05:04.490
and with the fullness of my strength.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.140
- Of course, the rest is history.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.950
Roosevelt was elected to
a fourth term in 1944,

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:10.670
shattering the two term custom.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.930
At the same time, in Great
Britain the war also prompted

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.870
a crisis of leadership.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.180
In 1940, Britain amended
its Parliament Act,

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.690
which had permitted a general
election every five years.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.230
Parliament instead opted to
extend Winston Churchill's

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.050
government for five more years.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:25.570
Although the war was Roosevelt's
primary justification

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:26.670
for serving four terms,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.540
not everyone was okay
with this development.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:31.220
Members of Congress had been
proposing term limit motions

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:34.420
for 140 years, and they continued to do so

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.020
during Roosevelt's time in office.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.570
Roosevelt was in office
from 1933 until 1945.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.720
Roosevelt died in office just 11 weeks

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:43.090
into his fourth term,

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.110
at which term Vice President
Harry Truman became President.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:49.260
In 1947, Congress passed
the 22nd Amendment

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.620
which limited the President to two

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:52.250
four-year terms in office.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.220
If was finally ratified
by the states in 1951.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.787
Now, the 22nd Amendment reads

00:05:56.787 --> 00:05:59.357
"No person shall be elected
to the office of the President

00:05:59.357 --> 00:06:01.547
"more than twice, and
no person who has held

00:06:01.547 --> 00:06:03.467
"the office of President,
or acted as President

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:05.707
"for more than two
years of a term to which

00:06:05.707 --> 00:06:08.537
"some other person was elected
President shall be elected

00:06:08.537 --> 00:06:10.910
"to the office of the
President more than once."

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.750
So the 22nd Amendment says
a person can only be elected

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:16.630
to be President two times
for a total of eight years.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.840
A person can serve for
a maximum of 10 years

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.630
in the case of a President
who is ascended to

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.130
the position as a Vice President.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.570
That means it's theoretically possible

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.260
for a President to serve more
than eight years in office.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.220
And a funny thing actually
happened while Congress

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.890
and the states were ratifying
the 22nd Amendment limiting

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.860
the number of years a person
can serve as President.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.470
In fact, President Truman tried
to win a third term in 1952.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:38.850
The amendment didn't apply to him,

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:41.010
since he was President
when the rule was passed,

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:43.487
because the second clause
in the 22nd Amendment reads

00:06:43.487 --> 00:06:45.597
"But this Article shall
not apply to any person

00:06:45.597 --> 00:06:47.597
"holding the office of
President when this Article

00:06:47.597 --> 00:06:50.197
"was proposed by Congress,
and shall not prevent

00:06:50.197 --> 00:06:52.687
"any person who may be holding
the office of President

00:06:52.687 --> 00:06:55.537
"or acting as President
during the term within

00:06:55.537 --> 00:06:57.867
"which this Article becomes
operative from holding

00:06:57.867 --> 00:06:59.887
"the office of President
or acting as President

00:06:59.887 --> 00:07:01.800
"during the remainder of such term."

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.220
So that gave Truman the
green light to run again.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:07.270
But Truman dropped his bid when he lost

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:08.910
the New Hampshire primary.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.250
But, of course, because you're
watching this video right now

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.780
this amendment did not settle
the debate over term limits.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:15.420
And Truman wasn't the last President

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:16.690
to consider a third term.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.230
So the question is what
happens if a President dies,

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.870
resigns, or is impeached?

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:23.810
The 22nd Amendment has
a provision covering

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.610
what happens if the Vice
President is forced to take over.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.820
If a Vice President takes over
because the President dies,

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.920
is forced to resign, or is impeached,

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.393
then serves less than two years,

00:07:33.393 --> 00:07:36.830
then the person may serve
for two terms as President.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.240
So for example, if
Donald Trump were to die

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.070
between now and January of 2021,

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:45.410
which seems unlikely given that
he's the paragon of health,

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.340
then Mike Pence could take
over and run for two terms

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:52.260
as President serving out
roughly nine years as President.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.230
But if the President dies within two years

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.680
of getting the office, like FDR,

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.340
who died within 11 weeks
of getting his fourth term,

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.490
then the person who becomes
President can only serve out

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.730
the remaining portion
of that President's term

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.400
and seek re-election once.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.660
This would have been the situation for

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:09.640
Vice President Gerald Ford, who stepped in

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.720
after President Nixon resigned in 1973.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.960
Ford was only eligible
to be re-elected once.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.230
He pardoned Richard Nixon,

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.960
and lost the 1976
election to Jimmy Carter.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.340
And if you read the text
of the 22nd Amendment,

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.870
it seems to have some open questions.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:24.210
For example, does it only prevent

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:25.910
consecutive four year terms?

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:27.440
Can a person serve two terms,

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.230
return to private life, and
then come back for a third?

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:31.520
Well, the answer is no.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.070
A President cannot run for a third term

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.220
after skipping a term.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.600
The amendment does actually
count the years served,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.870
not concurrent terms.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.610
President Obama, for example,
has already served two terms,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:41.443
and eight years.

00:08:41.443 --> 00:08:44.040
He cannot run for two
more consecutive terms

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:44.873
later in his life,

00:08:44.873 --> 00:08:47.401
or even one more term later in his life.

00:08:47.401 --> 00:08:49.640
Now, some of President Trump's supports

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:51.410
have suggested that since he was impeached

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.330
by the House, but not
convicted in the Senate,

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.950
he's eligible to run for two more terms.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.890
The reasoning seems to
be that impeachment,

00:08:57.890 --> 00:08:59.610
even without removal from office,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.640
nullifies his first term in office.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.500
So what impact, if any,
does the impeachment process

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.900
have on a person's ability
to serve more than two terms?

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:08.870
Well, the constitutional
mechanism for impeachment

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.890
of a federal officer,
including a President,

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:14.040
is set forth by Article II,
Section 4 of the Constitution,

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.927
which reads, "the
President, Vice President,

00:09:15.927 --> 00:09:18.007
"and all civil officers
of the United States

00:09:18.007 --> 00:09:20.627
"shall be removed from
office on impeachment for,

00:09:20.627 --> 00:09:22.657
"and conviction of treason, bribery,

00:09:22.657 --> 00:09:24.500
"and other high crimes and misdemeanors."

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.810
The House did in fact
impeach President Trump.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.060
Don't listen to anyone that says

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:29.660
President Trump was not impeached.

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:30.640
He was.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.390
But the Senate did not
vote to convict and remove.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.870
Now, the Constitution
in Article I, Section 3

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.987
says that the political
consequences of impeachment

00:09:39.987 --> 00:09:43.847
"shall not extend further
than to removal of office,

00:09:43.847 --> 00:09:46.547
"and disqualification to
hold and enjoy any office

00:09:46.547 --> 00:09:49.850
"of honor, trust or profit
under the United States."

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:53.280
This means that if a President
is impeached and removed

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.410
his or her removal is the only

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.310
political consequence that they face.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:59.220
Now, the person may still
face criminal charges,

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.010
but that's not a political issue.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:02.660
Although, President
Trump and his supporters

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.360
have argued that impeachment nullifies

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:07.670
an election's results, this
is definitely not the case.

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.420
The only political
consequence of impeachment

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:10.810
are removal and disqualification,

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.320
which we'll get to in a minute.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:14.050
Impeachment doesn't operate to somehow

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:17.260
give the impeached person
more time in office.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.860
So President Trump's time
in office is not nullified,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.770
and he's still bound
by the 22nd Amendment.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.010
But what would happen if a President

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.170
was impeached and removed?

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.370
Could he or she run for a third term then?

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:29.730
Well, the text of Article
I, Section 3 seems to imply

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:32.470
that impeachment and conviction
disqualifies a person

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.150
from running for a high office later on

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:35.510
in their post-impeachment life.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.500
Section 3 says that in
addition to removal,

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:39.887
the only political
consequence of conviction is

00:10:39.887 --> 00:10:42.187
"disqualification to
hold and enjoy any office

00:10:42.187 --> 00:10:44.830
"of honor, trust, or profit
under the United States."

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:47.570
In 2015, the Congressional
Research Service explained

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.280
that the Senate would
need to vote separately

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.600
on whether a person is
disqualified from future office

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.687
in conjunction with the
impeachment trial, stating,

00:10:55.687 --> 00:10:57.987
"although removal from
office would appear to flow

00:10:57.987 --> 00:11:01.527
"automatically from conviction
on an article of impeachment,

00:11:01.527 --> 00:11:04.127
"a separate vote is necessary
should the Senate deem it

00:11:04.127 --> 00:11:06.409
"appropriate to disqualify
the individual convicted

00:11:06.409 --> 00:11:09.847
"from holding future federal
offices of public trust.

00:11:09.847 --> 00:11:13.047
"Approval of such a measure
requires only the support

00:11:13.047 --> 00:11:14.300
"of a simple majority."

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.140
The report concluded that even if a person

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:17.510
is removed from federal office,

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:19.400
they may still hold another high office,

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.320
even President, if voters elect them.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.410
The Senate has used
this procedure to decide

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.530
whether other federal
office holders, like judges,

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:28.290
should be barred from
future federal offices.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.130
If applied in the Presidential context,

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.370
the Senate could vote by
majority to disqualify

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.550
a convicted President from future office.

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:36.700
There's an additional
wrinkle to disqualification.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.004
The Constitution isn't clear
on which public offices

00:11:39.004 --> 00:11:42.500
are those of "honor, trust, or profit."

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.680
You might conclude that
offices of Senator,

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:46.650
Representative, or
President are such offices.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:48.930
Or you can conclude exactly the opposite.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.220
This has not been settled, or even tested.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:52.900
The Congressional Research Office

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:54.880
is not necessarily the final word.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:57.530
That would be with the
Supreme Court presumably.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:59.690
And maybe this section of
the Constitution merely means

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.790
a person is disqualified
for federal offices,

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:04.460
but is free to run for state office.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.560
But the bottom line is this.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.850
If the Senate convicts the
President and doesn't hold

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:09.910
the crucial follow-up
vote on disqualification,

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:13.030
that person is free to
run for another office.

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:14.880
This may include the presidency,

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.920
but only if the person has
not served two full terms.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.660
Then that's all well and
good about the presidency.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.180
But maybe the vice
presidency is an end run

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:24.550
around the 22nd Amendment.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:26.140
So the first question
is do Vice President's

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:27.410
even have term limits?

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:28.840
The answer is actually no.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:31.400
Vice President's are not
subjected to term limits.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:33.420
They are treated the same
as members of Congress,

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:36.310
who also have no limits on
how long they can serve.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.290
Now, Mike Pence might
never become President.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.180
But let's say he serves
out eight full years

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:43.280
with President Trump,
and then audible shudder,

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:45.870
Ivanka Trump becomes President in 2024.

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:48.980
Could Mike Pence then serve
as her Vice President?

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:50.500
The answer is yes.

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:53.290
If Mike Pence wants to
become a kind of permanent

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.430
Vice President President sitter,

00:12:55.430 --> 00:12:56.820
there's nothing in the Constitution

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:58.170
that would prevent this scenario.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.600
In fact, this has actually happened twice.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:01.800
George Clinton.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.710
No, not that George Clinton,
and not that Parliament.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:07.880
It was George Clinton,
who was the Vice President

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:10.820
for both Thomas Jefferson
and James Madison.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:13.680
Similarly, John C. Calhoun
served as under both

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:16.150
John Quincy Adams and Andrew Jackson.

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:17.830
So we know that Vice President's can serve

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:19.160
more or less indefinitely.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.310
But there's one hypothetical
that certain parts

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:23.110
of the internet have really glommed onto.

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:26.070
There might be one
actual way to get around

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:28.880
the limitations of the 22nd Amendment.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:31.680
That involves the office
of vice presidency.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.710
It might be possible for an ex-President

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:35.810
to become Vice President and actually get

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.687
more time in office than
they normally would.

00:13:37.687 --> 00:13:38.920
But the first question is,

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:42.510
can an ex-President even
serve as Vice President?

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.180
The answer is yes.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.600
This seems like the last
thing any ex-President

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:47.580
would actually want to do.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.400
But a President is not barred from

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:51.550
later deciding to be the Vice President.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:53.550
No former President has ever volunteered

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.080
to be Vice President, but there's nothing

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.640
in the Constitution that would stop it.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:59.900
So given that a former
President can actually

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:03.010
be Vice President, one way
to potentially get around

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:06.700
the 22nd Amendment would
be for the President

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:09.310
to step down after their second full term,

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.890
to then serve as someone
else's Vice President,

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.359
and then for that President to step down,

00:14:15.359 --> 00:14:19.470
or resign, or be
impeached, or to just die.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.500
At that point the Vice
President would then

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.900
attain the office of presidency.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:27.050
So in that situation, the
President turned Vice President,

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:30.202
turned President again wouldn't be elected

00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:33.490
to the office of
President more than twice.

00:14:33.490 --> 00:14:36.420
But they would be able
to serve out more than

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:37.570
the mere eight years.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.650
And if you couple that
with becoming President

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:41.870
after starting out as Vice President,

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.340
that could be 10 years already,

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:46.800
you could add almost four
years on top of that.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:49.520
But hopefully that
situation never happens.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:51.240
It's a constitutional ambiguity,

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:53.800
and hopefully we won't
have to deal with that.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.740
But I, for one, don't
understand why any President

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.050
would want to run for a third term,

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.200
because being President is temporary.

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:01.630
But being a YouTube star is forever.

